  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon shows central Illinois \npositioned between an expansive area of high pressure centered over \nthe Mid-Atlantic states and low pressure over the northern plains. \nSouthwest flow between these two features has resulted in breezy \nsouth winds here locally with peak wind gusts up around 20-25 mph \nexpected through the remainder of the afternoon. Southerly flow will \nkeep unseasonably mild temperatures in place through the start of \nthe weekend with highs ranging from the 60s to near 70.\n\nThe next weather system to impact the area will begin approaching \nlate in the week as a few shortwave impulses eject north from the \nsouthwest US. A surface low will develop in the lee of the Rockies \nand lift through the Plains states and then Ontario early this \nweekend, sending a cold front through the Midwest states late \nSaturday night into Sunday. Light rain showers will accompany the \nfront, though lack of moisture from being so far displaced from the \nparent low will result in light accumulations. There is a \n40-50% chance that any given location will exceed 0.25\" of rain \n(NBM), with the highest chances in west-central Illinois.\n\nMuch cooler temperatures filter in behind the front for the start of \nthe new week with daytime highs only in the 40s with overnight lows \nin the 20s. Differences in pattern evolution exist heading into next \nweek, leading to uncertainty in additional precipitation chances.",
